PUBLIC RESPONSE: The Tonga flag consists of a red field, a white rectangle (canton) in the upper left, and a red cross inside that canton. Assigning value \(a\) to every red pixel and \(b\) to every white pixel produces a matrix whose rows take on at most three distinct values:

- rows lying entirely in the red field (or coinciding with the horizontal arm of the cross) are the constant vector of all \(a\)s;
- rows inside the canton but outside the cross are \(b\)s across the canton width followed by \(a\)s;
- rows that intersect the vertical arm of the cross are \(b\)s, then a short block of \(a\)s, then \(b\)s, then \(a\)s.

Hence the row space has dimension at most 3. For generic choices of \(a,b\) and of the flag proportions these three vectors are linearly independent, so the rank equals 3; the same bound holds at every resolution. Consequently the maximal attainable rank is exactly 3.

(The same conclusion follows from writing the matrix as \(bJ+(a-b)I\), where \(J\) is the all-ones matrix of rank 1 and \(I\) is the 0-1 indicator of the red regions; the geometry of Tonga’s red regions forces \(\operatorname{rank}(I)\le2\).)

