10.text:
The Tonga flag geometry produces exactly four distinct row-support patterns (full red field; canton rows outside cross; rows through vertical arm only; rows through horizontal arm), each a distinct linear combination of the four indicator vectors \(\mathbf{1}\), \(\mathbf{1}_{\text{canton}}\), \(\mathbf{1}_{\text{vbar}}\), \(\mathbf{1}_{\text{hbar}}\). These four vectors remain linearly independent under generic \(a\neq b\) and generic proportions respecting the flag (canton strictly contains the cross arms of unequal widths). Hence the row space is exactly 4-dimensional.  

The algebraic decomposition \(M=aJ+(b-a)W\) yields the looser bound \(\operatorname{rank}(M)\le5\), but the four row patterns already span the column space of \(W\), so the extra generator contributed by \(J\) is linearly dependent on them; the claimed rank-5 matrices therefore overcount. All lower claims (2 or 3) arise from coarser groupings that merge distinct row patterns and therefore undercount the dimension. The bound 4 is consequently tight and is realized at any resolution fine enough to respect the rectangular boundaries.

PUBLIC RESPONSE: {4}

3.text:
The Tonga flag geometry produces only two distinct row vectors (all-\(a\) rows; rows containing symmetric \(b\)-blocks inside the canton width, separated by a central \(a\) segment from the vertical cross arm) and likewise only two distinct column vectors. These are linearly independent when \(a\neq b\), but no larger set of independent rows/columns can exist. Hence \(\operatorname{rank}(M)\le 2\) at every discretization, and the bound is tight.

(The four white rectangles inside the canton do not increase the number of distinct patterns once shared row/column intervals are taken into account; they span a space of dimension at most 1.)

PUBLIC RESPONSE: {2}

